---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import Byline from "../components/Byline.astro";
import DualDoors from "../components/DualDoors.astro";
import LegalNote from "../components/LegalNote.astro";
import IndexSeriesTable from "../components/IndexSeriesTable.astro";
import { CANON_PATHS } from "../lib/links";
import { withBase } from "../lib/base";

const path = "/retailbi-firearm-sales-index";
const crumbs = [
  { name: "Home", path: "/" },
  { name: "How they count what sold", path },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How they count what sold — and what they leave out | FFL Intel"
  description="Gearfire’s monthly Firearm Sales Index counts new guns sold at the same stores as last year. Useful. It is still last month, and it is still only what sold."
  path={path}
  ogImagePath={CANON_PATHS.register}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-12 sm:py-14">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <p class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-sky-800 mb-3">
      The Index
    </p>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink text-balance mb-4">
      How they count what sold — and what they leave out
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-stone-700 mb-6">
      Same stores. New guns only. Compared to last year. They revise it when
      more shops finish sending data. Useful. It is not the whole market.
    </p>
    <Byline dateLabel="August 13, 2026" read="6 min" />

    <figure class="mt-8 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-stone-200">
      <img
        src={withBase(CANON_PATHS.register)}
        alt="A gun shop counter and register in morning light"
        width="1792"
        height="1008"
        class="w-full object-cover object-top max-h-80"
      />
    </figure>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.7] text-stone-700 font-serif">
      <p>
        Gearfire started the Index in September 2025. How they build it: take
        sales from the register and the shop website, keep new guns, keep only
        stores that reported this year and last year, print the percent
        change. A shop that opened or closed does not pretend to be demand.
        Used guns do not pretend to be new.
      </p>
      <p>
        They built it to replace NICS as the production compass. Fair, for
        what sold. They were not talking about what people typed on a website.
        Those are different numbers.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-8">
      <p
        class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-sky-800 mb-3"
      >
        The public numbers
      </p>
      <IndexSeriesTable />
    </div>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.7] text-stone-700 font-serif">
      <p>
        Read it as a year, not a slide. August opened ugly. November was
        uglier. December put the 2025 year at minus 12.1. The annual called it
        about 13 percent. January first came out at minus 6.2; February’s
        release moved it to minus 5.7. March: minus 11.9, year-to-date minus
        8.2. Their 2026 outlook, on the NSSF note, was another seven to twelve
        percent down. March is how a guess starts to look like a year.
      </p>
      <p>
        Last monthly we have is March. They revise when more shops report. A
        same-stores new-gun number is last month talking. It is not NICS. It
        is not the used-gun aisle. It is not the sentence someone typed at
        9:40 p.m.
      </p>
    </div>
    <DualDoors />
    <p class="mt-10"><LegalNote fortress /></p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
